752
00:40:16,530 --> 00:40:17,430
It's that important.

753
00:40:17,450 --> 00:40:18,210
Wow, number one.

754
00:40:18,670 --> 00:40:19,370
That's huge.

755
00:40:19,790 --> 00:40:25,060
Because if people hold back ideas or hide mistakes, you lose so much potential for learning and innovation.

756
00:40:25,040 --> 00:40:25,800
Exactly.

757
00:40:26,020 --> 00:40:28,700
And the sources suggest leaders need to model the behavior.

758
00:40:29,060 --> 00:40:30,460
Be open about your own challenges.

759
00:40:30,840 --> 00:40:32,050
Admit when you make a mistake.

760
00:40:32,590 --> 00:40:34,450
Share a time you learned from failure.

761
00:40:34,890 --> 00:40:35,990
That makes it OK for others.

762
00:40:36,110 --> 00:40:38,260
It normalizes learning, reduces that fear.

763
00:40:38,880 --> 00:40:42,460
It's also about how you respond when people share difficulties positively.

764
00:40:42,880 --> 00:40:45,660
Show that honesty is valued more than pretending everything's perfect.

765
00:40:45,840 --> 00:40:47,850
This needs a certain communication style, right?

766
00:40:48,390 --> 00:40:51,310
Empathetic, not judging, focused on solutions.

767
00:40:51,380 --> 00:40:54,840
So it's not just being vulnerable yourself, it's creating the space for others, too.

768
00:40:55,340 --> 00:40:58,670
And active listening must be critical here, showing you really understand.

769
00:40:59,090 --> 00:40:59,890
Absolutely critical.

770
00:41:00,330 --> 00:41:03,340
Active listening techniques, like, uh, paraphrasing.

771
00:41:03,900 --> 00:41:07,890
So what I hear you saying is you're concerned about X because of Y, is that right?

772
00:41:07,860 --> 00:41:11,290
Or summarizing key points, asking clarifying questions.

773
00:41:11,770 --> 00:41:14,760
Could you give me an example of what you mean by lack of clarity?

774
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:16,760
That really validates their input.

775
00:41:17,220 --> 00:41:19,660
Also setting ground rules for respectful discussion.

776
00:41:20,040 --> 00:41:21,910
Maybe even create them with the team.

777
00:41:22,350 --> 00:41:26,230
And simple stuff like celebrating successes together, even small wins.

778
00:41:26,370 --> 00:41:28,960
That builds positive vibes and team cohesion.

779
00:41:29,160 --> 00:41:29,400
Right.

780
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:36,590
So the big takeaway here seems to be that investing in your team's emotional well-being, acknowledging contributions properly.

781
00:41:37,070 --> 00:41:38,960
